\newlabel{tab:matrix}{{I}{3}{The run matrix: $111$ evolutions, grouped. Every group varies one knob against a named reference; per-run lines live in the shipped registry. Body parameters are $a=2$, $m=1$ ($R_{\min }=3.89$), $\sigma =-1$ and $d=12$ unless stated}{table.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces The undeclared seed: one exact throat, two resolutions, two fates. (a)~Minimal areal radius of the lone drainhole evolved from exact static data at refinement levels 2--4. The exact solution is a fixed point ($R_\star $, dotted); each level holds it until its own truncation error excites the unstable mode, and the fate flips with the grid: level 3 (dashed) collapses, level 4 (solid) inflates. Level 2 (grey) dies of an origin failure at $t=24.2$ (cross) with the throat still exact to $0.12\%$---too coarse to reach the branch at all. Dot and muted line as in Fig.~\ref {fig:seed_branches}: the scan loses the level-3 throat at $t=65$ while the run continues to $t=100$ behind a marginally trapped surface (areal radius $3.23\to 2.37$). (b)~The deviation grows exponentially on both grids: plateau fits over $t=49$--$61$ give e-fold times $\tau =5.88$ (level 3) and $5.26$ (level 4)---the same unstable mode to $9\%$, $T=\tau \,\alpha _{\rm  th}/R_\star =0.87$ and $0.78$ against the Gonz\'alez--Guzm\'an--Sarbach band $0.68$--$0.76$~\cite  {gonzalez2009}---with opposite sign. The fate is the truncation seed's; the mode is the throat's.}}{4}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:branches}{{1}{4}{The undeclared seed: one exact throat, two resolutions, two fates. (a)~Minimal areal radius of the lone drainhole evolved from exact static data at refinement levels 2--4. The exact solution is a fixed point ($R_\star $, dotted); each level holds it until its own truncation error excites the unstable mode, and the fate flips with the grid: level 3 (dashed) collapses, level 4 (solid) inflates. Level 2 (grey) dies of an origin failure at $t=24.2$ (cross) with the throat still exact to $0.12\%$---too coarse to reach the branch at all. Dot and muted line as in Fig.~\ref {fig:seed_branches}: the scan loses the level-3 throat at $t=65$ while the run continues to $t=100$ behind a marginally trapped surface (areal radius $3.23\to 2.37$). (b)~The deviation grows exponentially on both grids: plateau fits over $t=49$--$61$ give e-fold times $\tau =5.88$ (level 3) and $5.26$ (level 4)---the same unstable mode to $9\%$, $T=\tau \,\alpha _{\rm th}/R_\star =0.87$ and $0.78$ against the Gonz\'alez--Guzm\'an--Sarbach band $0.68$--$0.76$~\cite {gonzalez2009}---with opposite sign. The fate is the truncation seed's; the mode is the throat's}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Declared-seed branching of the lone throat (level 3). Areal throat radius of the drainhole ($a=2$, $m=1$; $R_\star =3.8895$, dotted) under the kick of Eq.~\eqref  {eq:seed}: $\varepsilon =\pm 10^{-2}$ (heavy), $\pm 10^{-3}$ (light); dashed = outward ($\varepsilon >0$). The fate is opposite to the push, and each pair crosses itself at the $\varepsilon $-independent $t_\times \simeq 13.0$---linear response. Burgundy: exponential fits $|R-R_\star |\propto e^{t/\tau }$, $\tau =4.5$ ($t=18$--$31$) and $4.0$ ($t=16$--$26$)---steeper than the unkicked mode's $5.88$ (Fig.~\ref {fig:branches}) while the kick's transient decays. Dots: the scan loses the throat (the inflating runs continue to $t=100$, still growing); cross: death at $t=40.1$. The $\varepsilon =+10^{-2}$ arm holds a horizon from $t=11$ (areal radius $3.88\to 2.34\to 2.57$) and runs clean to $t=100$; the muted line carries its last reading. A tenfold smaller kick arrives $8$--$12$ units later.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:seed_branches}{{2}{4}{Declared-seed branching of the lone throat (level 3). Areal throat radius of the drainhole ($a=2$, $m=1$; $R_\star =3.8895$, dotted) under the kick of Eq.~\eqref {eq:seed}: $\varepsilon =\pm 10^{-2}$ (heavy), $\pm 10^{-3}$ (light); dashed = outward ($\varepsilon >0$). The fate is opposite to the push, and each pair crosses itself at the $\varepsilon $-independent $t_\times \simeq 13.0$---linear response. Burgundy: exponential fits $|R-R_\star |\propto e^{t/\tau }$, $\tau =4.5$ ($t=18$--$31$) and $4.0$ ($t=16$--$26$)---steeper than the unkicked mode's $5.88$ (Fig.~\ref {fig:branches}) while the kick's transient decays. Dots: the scan loses the throat (the inflating runs continue to $t=100$, still growing); cross: death at $t=40.1$. The $\varepsilon =+10^{-2}$ arm holds a horizon from $t=11$ (areal radius $3.88\to 2.34\to 2.57$) and runs clean to $t=100$; the muted line carries its last reading. A tenfold smaller kick arrives $8$--$12$ units later}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Attraction and repulsion switched by one sign ($d=12$, released from rest). (a)~Separation change of three pairs one knob apart: the like-oriented pair ($\sigma =+1$, dashed) pushes apart; flipping one field ($\sigma =-1$, solid) turns the push into a pull; the narrow like pair ($a=1$, light) opens $2.9\times $ more slowly---the force follows the throat width (the scalar charge), not the mass, which is equal in all three. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits, sub-cell where the mouth tracker's $0.03$ grid quantum would swamp the early motion. (b)~The flipped pair's infall over the like pair's escape: with $Q \equiv |F_\phi /F_{\rm  grav}| = 5$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:binary}), pull and push are $(Q{+}1)$ and $(Q{-}1)$, predicting $3/2$ (burgundy); measured $1.52\pm 0.02$ over $t=3.5$--$10.5$. Coordinates under-read the motion while the gauge settles (band), but that systematic is common to both arms and cancels in the ratio: the two runs differ in exactly one parameter.}}{5}{figure.3}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:sign_rule}{{3}{5}{Attraction and repulsion switched by one sign ($d=12$, released from rest). (a)~Separation change of three pairs one knob apart: the like-oriented pair ($\sigma =+1$, dashed) pushes apart; flipping one field ($\sigma =-1$, solid) turns the push into a pull; the narrow like pair ($a=1$, light) opens $2.9\times $ more slowly---the force follows the throat width (the scalar charge), not the mass, which is equal in all three. Separations are inverse-$\chi $-weighted centroids of the midplane $\chi $ pits, sub-cell where the mouth tracker's $0.03$ grid quantum would swamp the early motion. (b)~The flipped pair's infall over the like pair's escape: with $Q \equiv |F_\phi /F_{\rm grav}| = 5$ (Sec.~\ref {sec:model:binary}), pull and push are $(Q{+}1)$ and $(Q{-}1)$, predicting $3/2$ (burgundy); measured $1.52\pm 0.02$ over $t=3.5$--$10.5$. Coordinates under-read the motion while the gauge settles (band), but that systematic is common to both arms and cancels in the ratio: the two runs differ in exactly one parameter}{figure.3}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Placement is on the ruler; the squeeze is not. (a)~Per-mouth areal radius of two exact drainhole throats placed at rest, read at $t=0$ by one-step probes at separations $d=6$--$48$ (ink): the pair reads wider than the isolated throat ($R_\star $, dotted), the excess falling as $d^{-1.16}$---the neighbour's field on the ruler, not a dynamical widening. Burgundy squares: the head-on scout's mouths (the $\sigma =-1$ pair released from rest at $d=8$, Sec.~\ref {sec:headon}) at the separation it had reached; open squares, here and in (b): the scout closer than the closest probe. (b)~The scout against the curve at its own separation, $\delta R/R = R_{\mathrm  {scout}}/R_{\mathrm  {place}}-1$. The $-0.13\%$ plateau to $t=5$ is the mouth tracker's grid snap ($d$ reads $7.94$ for a true $8$) and calibrates the systematic; the throats' own squeeze then grows to $-4.0\%$ by $t=13$ ($d=6.1$). In the band the curve is held at its last point, so the magnitude is a lower bound---the apparent recovery is the ruler's, not the throats'. A boost can only raise a coordinate-sphere reading, so a reading below the curve is a real squeeze, if anything underestimated.}}{5}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:placement}{{4}{5}{Placement is on the ruler; the squeeze is not. (a)~Per-mouth areal radius of two exact drainhole throats placed at rest, read at $t=0$ by one-step probes at separations $d=6$--$48$ (ink): the pair reads wider than the isolated throat ($R_\star $, dotted), the excess falling as $d^{-1.16}$---the neighbour's field on the ruler, not a dynamical widening. Burgundy squares: the head-on scout's mouths (the $\sigma =-1$ pair released from rest at $d=8$, Sec.~\ref {sec:headon}) at the separation it had reached; open squares, here and in (b): the scout closer than the closest probe. (b)~The scout against the curve at its own separation, $\delta R/R = R_{\mathrm {scout}}/R_{\mathrm {place}}-1$. The $-0.13\%$ plateau to $t=5$ is the mouth tracker's grid snap ($d$ reads $7.94$ for a true $8$) and calibrates the systematic; the throats' own squeeze then grows to $-4.0\%$ by $t=13$ ($d=6.1$). In the band the curve is held at its last point, so the magnitude is a lower bound---the apparent recovery is the ruler's, not the throats'. A boost can only raise a coordinate-sphere reading, so a reading below the curve is a real squeeze, if anything underestimated}{figure.4}{}}
