\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces The waveform gallery: every channel's radiation, one row per scenario, one retarded clock. Left column: $r\,\mathrm  {Re}\,\Psi _4$ of the dominant mode at the innermost extraction sphere, each row on its own stated scale---the lone throat radiates an order of magnitude below the mergers, and the fly-by is the loudest channel of all. Half-height caps mark record ends: the collapsing throat's stream is junk-gated at $t=70$, the spiral record ends at $t=60$ with the run still in flight, and the fly-by is gated at $t=70$, where the core's late disturbance reaches the $R=14$ sphere (its close-out runs continue). Right column: the envelope $|r\Psi _4|$ at every extraction sphere on its own retarded time (ink to faint, inner to outer): a signal travelling at $c$ and falling as $1/r$ folds onto one curve. Burgundy dots mark the correlated wavefront, and the quoted $v/c$ is the lag of the whole complex waveform between neighbouring spheres over their common retarded window---peak-to-peak timing fails on flat-topped envelopes (the vacuum twin's merger plateau is ${\sim }15$ units wide at both spheres, and its peak times alone would read $v=0.57c$ where the waveform lag reads $0.82c$). Measured speeds: throat $0.91/0.95/0.98$, head-on $0.96/0.90$, spiral $1.00/1.00/1.00$, fly-by $1.00$, vacuum twin $0.82$. Every channel propagates at $c$ within the output cadence: radiation, not a gauge or constraint mode.}}{7}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gw_gallery}{{5}{7}{The waveform gallery: every channel's radiation, one row per scenario, one retarded clock. Left column: $r\,\mathrm {Re}\,\Psi _4$ of the dominant mode at the innermost extraction sphere, each row on its own stated scale---the lone throat radiates an order of magnitude below the mergers, and the fly-by is the loudest channel of all. Half-height caps mark record ends: the collapsing throat's stream is junk-gated at $t=70$, the spiral record ends at $t=60$ with the run still in flight, and the fly-by is gated at $t=70$, where the core's late disturbance reaches the $R=14$ sphere (its close-out runs continue). Right column: the envelope $|r\Psi _4|$ at every extraction sphere on its own retarded time (ink to faint, inner to outer): a signal travelling at $c$ and falling as $1/r$ folds onto one curve. Burgundy dots mark the correlated wavefront, and the quoted $v/c$ is the lag of the whole complex waveform between neighbouring spheres over their common retarded window---peak-to-peak timing fails on flat-topped envelopes (the vacuum twin's merger plateau is ${\sim }15$ units wide at both spheres, and its peak times alone would read $v=0.57c$ where the waveform lag reads $0.82c$). Measured speeds: throat $0.91/0.95/0.98$, head-on $0.96/0.90$, spiral $1.00/1.00/1.00$, fly-by $1.00$, vacuum twin $0.82$. Every channel propagates at $c$ within the output cadence: radiation, not a gauge or constraint mode}{figure.5}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Every source on a detector, at one shared calibration: total mass $M=30\,M_\odot $, distance $D=10$ Mpc; strain scales as $M/D$ and frequency as $1/M$. (a)~Strain amplitude spectral densities of the innermost-sphere records over the aLIGO design floor (burgundy). Every channel clears the floor around $100$ Hz: the fly-by peaks at $2.1\times 10^{-20}\,\mathrm  {Hz}^{-1/2}$ ($96$ Hz); the head-on, spiral and vacuum twin lie within a factor of two of one another ($3.4$--$5.6\times 10^{-21}$); the lone collapsing throat sits an order below ($3.9\times 10^{-22}$). The faint straight line is the analytic Newtonian inspiral of the same equal-mass binary, $|\tilde  h(f)|\propto \mathcal  {M}^{5/6}f^{-7/6}$, drawn to its ISCO at $147$ Hz through the same normalisation as the numerical record: the ramp an astrophysical binary rides into the band where the simulated signal begins. (b)~Peak-frequency tracks of the Morlet wavelet transform of each record (the line's width is the signal's own envelope; tracks are drawn only above $0.2$ of each record's peak and outside the cone of influence). The shapes separate the sources: the collapsing throat rings in an arch at $280$--$334$ Hz, the head-on bell slows from $210$ to $170$ Hz, the vacuum twin climbs through merger onto a $227$ Hz ringdown shelf, and the spiral and fly-by (records in flight) enter as rising arcs at $165$--$195$ Hz.}}{8}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:gw_ligo}{{6}{8}{Every source on a detector, at one shared calibration: total mass $M=30\,M_\odot $, distance $D=10$ Mpc; strain scales as $M/D$ and frequency as $1/M$. (a)~Strain amplitude spectral densities of the innermost-sphere records over the aLIGO design floor (burgundy). Every channel clears the floor around $100$ Hz: the fly-by peaks at $2.1\times 10^{-20}\,\mathrm {Hz}^{-1/2}$ ($96$ Hz); the head-on, spiral and vacuum twin lie within a factor of two of one another ($3.4$--$5.6\times 10^{-21}$); the lone collapsing throat sits an order below ($3.9\times 10^{-22}$). The faint straight line is the analytic Newtonian inspiral of the same equal-mass binary, $|\tilde h(f)|\propto \mathcal {M}^{5/6}f^{-7/6}$, drawn to its ISCO at $147$ Hz through the same normalisation as the numerical record: the ramp an astrophysical binary rides into the band where the simulated signal begins. (b)~Peak-frequency tracks of the Morlet wavelet transform of each record (the line's width is the signal's own envelope; tracks are drawn only above $0.2$ of each record's peak and outside the cone of influence). The shapes separate the sources: the collapsing throat rings in an arch at $280$--$334$ Hz, the head-on bell slows from $210$ to $170$ Hz, the vacuum twin climbs through merger onto a $227$ Hz ringdown shelf, and the spiral and fly-by (records in flight) enter as rising arcs at $165$--$195$ Hz}{figure.6}{}}
